From: meta-aws-demos <noreply@example.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] binman: use importlib.resources instead of pkg_resources

setuptools >= 80 (wrynose ships 82.0.1) removed the legacy pkg_resources
module, so binman's `import pkg_resources` fails at build time with
"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pkg_resources'".

control.py already imports importlib.resources (with an importlib_resources
fallback), so migrate the two remaining pkg_resources.resource_* calls to the
importlib.resources API and drop the pkg_resources import. Resources live in
the binman package (binman/missing-blob-help and binman/etype/).

This mirrors the same migration made in upstream U-Boot
(tools/binman/control.py on u-boot master, which uses
importlib_resources.files(__package__)...). u-boot-fslc 2025.01 predates it.

Upstream-Status: Backport [equivalent to u-boot master tools/binman/control.py]
---
 tools/binman/control.py | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tools/binman/control.py b/tools/binman/control.py
index 0000000..1111111 100644
--- a/tools/binman/control.py
+++ b/tools/binman/control.py
@@ -13,7 +13,6 @@ except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover
     # for Python 3.6
     import importlib_resources
 import os
-import pkg_resources
 import re
 
 import sys
@@ -96,7 +95,8 @@ def _ReadMissingBlobHelp():
             msg = ''
         return tag, msg
 
-    my_data = pkg_resources.resource_string(__name__, 'missing-blob-help')
+    my_data = importlib.resources.files('binman').joinpath(
+        'missing-blob-help').read_bytes()
     re_tag = re.compile('^([-a-z0-9]+):$')
     result = {}
     tag = None
@@ -151,7 +151,8 @@ def GetEntryModules(include_testing=True):
     Returns:
         Set of paths to entry class filenames
     """
-    glob_list = pkg_resources.resource_listdir(__name__, 'etype')
+    glob_list = [entry.name
+                 for entry in importlib.resources.files('binman.etype').iterdir()]
     glob_list = [fname for fname in glob_list if fname.endswith('.py')]
     return set([os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(item))[0]
                 for item in glob_list
